concern:
  heading: "Participant privacy and audit defensibility"
  body: >
    <p>Two questions come up immediately and both deserve a straight answer. On privacy:
    participant information is sensitive information under the Privacy Act, and pasting
    it into a consumer chatbot is not acceptable practice. The program covers which tools
    have terms that make business use defensible, what must be de-identified before it
    goes anywhere, and how to set up a workflow where the sensitive detail never leaves
    your systems.</p>
    <p>On audit: AI drafts, a worker verifies and signs. That is the same accountability
    model as a support worker writing notes from memory, except the draft is more complete
    and it gets written on the day. What we train people to avoid is the failure mode that
    matters here — signing something you didn't read because it looked plausible.</p>
faq:
  - q: "Does AI-assisted documentation meet NDIS Commission expectations?"
    a: "The Commission's concern is that records are accurate, contemporaneous and attributable to the worker — not which tool produced the first draft. A verified, signed note written on the day is stronger evidence than one reconstructed from memory a week later. We train the verification step explicitly, because that is where the obligation actually lives. Your own quality team should sign off the workflow before you roll it out."
  - q: "Our staff aren't office workers. Will they use this?"
    a: "Most of the value lands on a phone, which is where support workers already are. The program teaches capture-and-draft workflows that start from a voice note or three lines of rough text after a shift, rather than assuming anyone is sitting at a desk."
  - q: "Will this be used to cut hours?"
    a: "That's a fair thing to ask and worth being direct about: this compresses documentation time, not support time. Providers we talk to are trying to stop unpaid after-hours writing and reduce turnover, not reduce billable support. If the goal were headcount, we'd be the wrong provider."
  - q: "Can it write in the participant's communication style?"
    a: "Yes, and it's one of the more useful things it does — the same information at the reading level and in the format a particular participant or family can take in. That's a real accessibility gain, not a cosmetic one."
  - q: "We're a small provider with 15 staff. Is this viable?"
    a: "Yes. Documentation load per worker is often higher in small providers because there is no back office absorbing it. The program is the same three months regardless of team size."
